@anon.5c04a1d5f2b09caa3146db3fe8360175.958 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2591 x i8] }> <{ [2591 x i8] c"A repeating pattern fill.\0A\0ATypst supports the most common pattern type of tiled patterns, where a\0Apattern is repeated in a grid-like fashion, covering the entire area of an\0Aelement that is filled or stroked. The pattern is defined by a tile size and\0Aa body defining the content of each cell. You can also add horizontal or\0Avertical spacing between the cells of the patterng.\0A\0A# Examples\0A\0A```example\0A#let pat = pattern(size: (30pt, 30pt))[\0A  #place(line(start: (0%, 0%), end: (100%, 100%)))\0A  #place(line(start: (0%, 100%), end: (100%, 0%)))\0A]\0A\0A#rect(fill: pat, width: 100%, height: 60pt, stroke: 1pt)\0A```\0A\0APatterns are also supported on text, but only when setting the\0A[relativeness]($pattern.relative) to either `{auto}` (the default value) or\0A`{\22parent\22}`. To create word-by-word or glyph-by-glyph patterns, you can\0Awrap the words or characters of your text in [boxes]($box) manually or\0Athrough a [show rule]($styling/#show-rules).\0A\0A```example\0A#let pat = pattern(\0A  size: (30pt, 30pt),\0A  relative: \22parent\22,\0A  square(\0A    size: 30pt,\0A    fill: gradient\0A      .conic(..color.map.rainbow),\0A  )\0A)\0A\0A#set text(fill: pat)\0A#lorem(10)\0A```\0A\0AYou can also space the elements further or closer apart using the\0A[`spacing`]($pattern.spacing) feature of the pattern. If the spacing\0Ais lower than the size of the pattern, the pattern will overlap.\0AIf it is higher, the pattern will have gaps of the same color as the\0Abackground of the pattern.\0A\0A```example\0A#let pat = pattern(\0A  size: (30pt, 30pt),\0A  spacing: (10pt, 10pt),\0A  relative: \22parent\22,\0A  square(\0A    size: 30pt,\0A    fill: gradient\0A     .conic(..color.map.rainbow),\0A  ),\0A)\0A\0A#rect(\0A  width: 100%,\0A  height: 60pt,\0A  fill: pat,\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Relativeness\0AThe location of the starting point of the pattern is dependent on the\0Adimensions of a container. This container can either be the shape that it is\0Abeing painted on, or the closest surrounding container. This is controlled\0Aby the `relative` argument of a pattern constructor. By default, patterns\0Aare relative to the shape they are being painted on, unless the pattern is\0Aapplied on text, in which case they are relative to the closest ancestor\0Acontainer.\0A\0ATypst determines the ancestor container as follows:\0A- For shapes that are placed at the root/top level of the document, the\0A  closest ancestor is the page itself.\0A- For other shapes, the ancestor is the innermost [`block`] or [`box`] that\0A  contains the shape. This includes the boxes and blocks that are implicitly\0A  created by show rules and elements. For example, a [`rotate`] will not\0A  affect the parent of a gradient, but a [`grid`] will." }>, align 1
